---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { displayHost, safeUrl } from '../../lib/format';
import { SIZE_INFO, SIZE_ORDER } from '../../lib/labels';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/urls';
import { loadSiteData } from '../../server/data';

const data = loadSiteData();
const sources = data.manifest.sources;
---

<Base title="About, sources and credits" description="How Card Show Atlas gathers its listings, how it estimates show size, and who deserves credit for the data." path="about/">
  <div class="container page">
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>About Card Show Atlas</h1>
      <p>
        Card Show Atlas is a free guide to trading card shows in the US and Canada: sports cards, Pokémon, Magic, Yu-Gi-Oh, One Piece, Lorcana and more. It is
        for people who want to know what is on near them this weekend, and for vendors who need to pick shows, remember deadlines and work out what a trip
        will cost.
      </p>
      <p class="notice notice-warn">
        <strong>Early preview.</strong> The list is still being built. Details can be wrong or out of date, so check the organizer's own page or post before
        you drive.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how">How the listings are gathered</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Public listings.</strong> Some listing sites and organizers publish their schedules. We read them automatically only when their rules and
          robots.txt allow it. Where they do not, we use them only as leads and confirm on the organizer's own page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Research.</strong> Automated scripts and AI research assistants look for missing details, such as dates, venues, table prices and
          application deadlines, and record the links they found them on. A person can correct anything they get wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You.</strong> Visitors and organizers send in shows and fixes. Every submission is checked before it is published.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We keep facts: names, dates, venues, prices. We never copy anyone's write up. Every show page names each source that listed it, links to it, and says
        when it was last checked. A show stays "Listed by" a site until the organizer's own page confirms it, and then it shows as "Confirmed by organizer".
      </p>
      <p>
        Listings refresh every day, and shows coming up in the next two months are re-checked more often. A show that no source has mentioned for a while
        gets a "may be out of date" note instead of disappearing. Past shows drop off the lists and the map, but stay in our records because they help us
        estimate crowds.
      </p>
      <p>
        Contact details are only the ones an organizer has published for the show: its own email, page or business phone. Never a personal number or a home
        address, even if a source shows one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="size">How show size is estimated</h2>
      <p>
        Most shows never say how many people come, so we estimate it, and we always say how. We use the best information we have, in this order:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>An attendance number from the organizer or the press.</li>
        <li>Player counts for tournaments.</li>
        <li>The number of vendor tables or booths.</li>
        <li>The size of the venue.</li>
        <li>History: how long the show has run and how past editions did.</li>
        <li>Entry price, number of days and kind of event.</li>
        <li>How many people live within about an hour's drive.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        From that we give each show a likely crowd with a low and a high number, and a label for how sure we are: <em>Reported</em>, <em>Estimated</em> or
        <em>Rough guess</em>. When a show lists its tables, the crowd is the tables times an "people per table" factor that depends on the kind of event and the
        number of days. We start from stated assumptions and refine them as more shows publish both numbers.
      </p>
      <table class="price-table">
        <caption class="sr-only">Size labels</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Label</th><th scope="col">Roughly</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {SIZE_ORDER.map((s) => <tr><td>{SIZE_INFO[s].label}</td><td>{SIZE_INFO[s].blurb}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted small">The exact cut points will be adjusted once real data arrives.</p>
      <p>
        Vendors also see <strong>traffic per table</strong>: the expected crowd divided by the number of tables. A huge show with 600 tables can be a worse
        room than a medium one with 60.
      </p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        Your location, your My shows list and your vendor settings stay on your device, in your browser. There are no accounts, no tracking and no analytics.
        Clearing your browser data deletes them, which is why My shows has a backup button.
      </p>

      <h2 id="licences">Licences and credits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The code is under the MIT licence. The show data is shared under CC BY 4.0.</li>
        <li>
          Maps use tiles and data from <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap contributors</a> (data under the Open Database
          Licence), drawn with <a href="https://leafletjs.com">Leaflet</a> and <a href="https://github.com/mapbox/supercluster">Supercluster</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          ZIP and postal code locations come from <a href="https://www.geonames.org">GeoNames</a> (CC BY 4.0).
        </li>
        <li>
          Population figures used for crowd estimates come from the US Census Bureau and Statistics Canada.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 id="sources">Listing sources</h3>
      {
        sources.length === 0 ? (
          <p class="muted">Every source used for the listings is credited here as soon as the first shows are loaded.</p>
        ) : (
          <ul>
            {sources.map((s) => (
              <li>
                {safeUrl(s.url) ? <a href={safeUrl(s.url)!}>{s.name}</a> : s.name}
                {s.url && safeUrl(s.url) ? <span class="muted"> ({displayHost(s.url)})</span> : ''}
                {s.credit ? <>. {s.credit}</> : ''}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )
      }
      {data.manifest.built && <p class="muted small">Data last built: {data.manifest.built.slice(0, 10)}.</p>}

      <h2 id="contact">Add, fix or remove</h2>
      <p>
        Missing a show, or spotted a mistake? See <a href={withBase('add/')}>Add or fix a show</a>. To ask for a show or some of its details to be removed,
        see <a href={withBase('add/#remove')}>removal requests</a>. Requests are honored.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
